# Media, journalism and communication, stages 0 to 5.
# Who made it, what does it show, what is the evidence, and what was left out?
# Posts, outlets, platforms and figures are invented for teaching.

@ media.0.messages
goal: Know that words, pictures, sounds and signs with the body can all carry a message.
intro: A message is something that one person tells another. We can send a message with words, with a picture, with a wave or with a sound. Someone sends it, and someone gets it.
  show: emoji 🗣️ 👋 🖼️ 🔔
demo: A child points to a picture of a drink. A grown-up asks, "Would you like some water?" The child nods. The picture sent the message, and the question checked what it meant.
  show: flow point to the picture > "Would you like water?" > nod: yes
M: Match each message to what it means.
  pairs: A wave => Hello or goodbye | A finger on the lips => Be quiet | A picture of a cup => Drinks go here | A ringing bell => Time to tidy up
  hint: What do you do when you say goodbye?
  why: Messages can be sent without any words.
G: How is the message sent?
  bin With your voice: saying "hello" | singing a song
  bin With your body: waving | nodding
  bin With a picture: a drawing | a sign on the door
  hint: Do you hear it, see someone do it, or look at it?
  why: Voices, bodies and pictures can all send messages.
P: Mum waves to you from across the road. Who is sending the message?
  + Mum :: She is the one waving.
  - You :: [sender-and-receiver-confused] You are the one who gets the message.
  - The road :: [not-a-person] A road cannot send a message.
  hint: Who is waving?
  why: The sender is the one who makes the message. You are the one who receives it.
>P: Your friend points at the toy shelf. You do not know which toy your friend wants. What can you do?
  + Hold one up and ask, "This one?" :: Asking checks what the message means.
  - Guess, and walk away :: [did-not-check] You might guess wrong.
  - Do nothing :: [ignored-the-message] Your friend was trying to tell you something.
  hint: How can you find out what your friend means?
  why: When a message is not clear, we can ask or show, to check.
done: Messages come in many ways. Send someone a message today without using any words.

@ media.0.pretend-real
goal: Tell a pretend story from something real, and say what a picture really shows.
intro: Some stories are made up for fun. Some tell about things that really happened. A talking moon is pretend. A photo of a walk in the park shows something real.
  show: emoji 🌙 🐱 📷
demo: In a cartoon, a cat flies to the moon holding a balloon. That is pretend, and it is fun! Then we see a real photo of a cat sitting by a window. We say only what we can see: "A cat is sitting by a window."
  show: table Picture | Pretend or real? ;; a cat flying to the moon | pretend ;; a cat sitting by a window | real
G: Pretend or real?
  bin Pretend: a cat flying to the moon | a talking teapot | a dragon at school
  bin Real: a cat sitting by a window | rain falling | a bus on the road
  hint: Could it really happen?
  why: Cats cannot fly, and teapots cannot talk. Those are make-believe.
P: A photo shows a cat sitting by a window. What does the photo really show?
  + A cat sitting by a window :: That is just what we can see.
  - A cat that is sad because it lost its toy :: [added-a-story] The photo does not show a lost toy. That is a story someone has added.
  - A cat that can fly :: [not-in-the-picture] The cat is sitting, not flying.
  hint: Say only what you can see.
  why: A picture shows what is in it. We should take care not to add things that are not there.
P: You see a picture that makes you feel worried or mixed up. What should you do?
  + Ask a grown-up you trust about it :: They can help you to work it out.
  - Keep it to yourself :: [did-not-ask] You do not have to work it out alone.
  - Believe it straight away :: [believed-without-checking] Some pictures are pretend, or have been changed.
  hint: Who can help you?
  why: A grown-up you trust can help when a picture is surprising or upsetting.
>P: In a film, a boy flies through the air on a broomstick. Is it pretend or real?
  + Pretend: people cannot fly on broomsticks :: It is a story made for fun.
  - Real: it is on the screen :: [screen-means-real] Films can show things that are made up.
  - Real: the boy looks real :: [looks-real-means-real] A real actor can be in a pretend story.
  hint: Can people really fly on broomsticks?
  why: Things on a screen can be pretend, even when the people in them look real.
done: Pretend can be fun, and real is what happened. Look at a picture and say only what you can see.

@ media.1.source-purpose
goal: Find out who made a message, who it is for and what it wants you to do.
intro: Every message is made by someone, for someone. Some messages tell you things. Some are for fun. Some try to get you to buy or choose something. Three questions help: who made it, who is it for, and what does it want me to do?
  show: table Question | It tells you ;; who made it? | the source ;; who is it for? | the audience ;; what does it want me to do? | the purpose
demo: A poster says that a toy is "the best ever!" It shows children smiling. The poster was made by the people who sell the toy, for people who might buy it. The smiles catch your eye, but they do not prove that the toy is the best one for you.
  show: flow who made it: the toy seller > who is it for: buyers > what does it want: for you to buy it
G: What is the message for?
  bin To tell you something: a notice that the pool is closed today | a weather forecast
  bin To be fun: a funny cartoon | a bedtime story
  bin To get you to choose something: an advertisement for a toy | a poster saying "Vote for Sam!"
  hint: Does it tell, amuse, or try to change what you choose?
  why: Messages can inform, entertain or persuade. Some do more than one of these.
M: Match each question to what it finds.
  pairs: Who made it? => The source | Who is it for? => The audience | What does it want me to do? => The purpose
  hint: The source is where the message comes from.
  why: These three questions are the start of understanding any message.
P: A poster is bright, with lovely pictures and friendly writing. Does that mean that what it says is true?
  + No: how a message looks does not tell you whether it is right :: You have to check what it says.
  - Yes: nice posters tell the truth :: [looks-as-proof] Anyone can make a poster look nice.
  - No: bright posters are never true :: [overcorrection] Some are true. How they look just does not tell you.
  hint: Can something untrue be made to look nice?
  why: Bright colours and smiling faces catch our eye. They do not show that a message is right.
>P: A cereal box shows a cartoon tiger and says, "Makes you super strong!" Who made the message, and why?
  + The cereal company, to get you to buy the cereal :: The tiger is there to catch your eye.
  - The tiger, to help you :: [character-as-source] The tiger is a drawing. A company made the box.
  - A doctor, to keep you well :: [wrong-source] The box was made by the people who sell the cereal.
  hint: Who earns money when the cereal is bought?
  why: Asking who made a message, and what they want, helps us to think before we believe it.
done: Who made it, who is it for, and what does it want? Ask the three questions about one advertisement today.

@ media.1.make-message
goal: Make a clear message for the people who will read it, and check before you share things about other people.
intro: A clear message tells people what they need to know. An invitation must say what is happening, and when, and where. Before we share, we ask: is it all right to show this? We ask first before using someone else's picture.
  show: chips what? | when? | where?
demo: A class makes an invitation to a puppet show. The first try has a big puppet on it, but no time! A partner asks, "When is it?" So the class adds the time and the place. They use their own drawings, and not photos of children.
  show: table Draft | What it says ;; first | a big puppet, and no time ;; second | Puppet show! Friday at 2 o'clock, in the hall
E: This invitation is missing something. Make it better.
  tokens: Puppet show! | Come soon.{Friday at 2 o'clock in the hall.,It will be fun.}
  limit: 1
  aim: Change one part so that people know when and where to come.
  accept: 2=Friday at 2 o'clock in the hall.
  x 2=It will be fun. :: [missing-details] That still does not say when or where.
  hint: What do people need to know in order to come?
  why: An invitation needs the day, the time and the place.
S: Choose everything that an invitation to a show must say.
  + What it is :: People need to know what they are invited to.
  + When it is :: They need to know the day and the time.
  + Where it is :: They need to know where to go.
  - The colour of the curtains :: [unneeded-detail] That does not help anyone to come.
  - What you had for lunch :: [unneeded-detail] That has nothing to do with the show.
  hint: What would you need to know in order to get there?
  why: What, when and where are the things that people need.
G: All right to put on a class poster, or keep it private?
  bin All right to put on the poster: the time of the show | the name of the show | a drawing that you made
  bin Keep it private: your home address | a photo of a friend, used without asking | your password
  hint: Would it be safe for anyone at all to see it?
  why: Some things are private. We ask before sharing anything about another person.
>P: You are making a poster for the school notice board about a glove that you have lost. What should it say?
  + What the glove looks like, and to hand it in at the school office :: People need to know what to look for, and where to take it.
  - Your home address, so that someone can bring it round :: [shared-private-details] Do not put your address on a poster. Use the school office.
  - Only "Help!" :: [missing-details] Nobody would know what was lost.
  hint: What does a finder need to know, and what should stay private?
  why: A good message gives the details that are needed and keeps private things private.
done: What, when and where, and ask before you share. Make an invitation and let a friend check that nothing is missing.

@ media.2.verification
goal: Check a claim: say exactly what it asserts, trace it to where it came from, and tell repetition from independent confirmation.
intro: To check a claim, first pin it down: what exactly is being said, about where, and when? Then trace it back to its origin. Many websites may repeat a single report. Fifty copies of one claim are still one claim.
  show: flow state the claim exactly > find its origin > check the date and the place > look for independent evidence
demo: Three posts say that the local pond has dried up. All three copy one article. That article is two years old, and is about a pond in a different town. The three posts together tell us nothing about our pond today.
  show: flow three posts > one article > two years old, and another town
E: Make this claim precise enough to check.
  tokens: The town pond | has problems{was completely dry on 3 June,is not what it used to be}
  limit: 1
  aim: Change the claim so that it says exactly what happened, and when.
  accept: 2=was completely dry on 3 June
  x 2=is not what it used to be :: [still-vague] That cannot be checked. It does not say what, or when.
  hint: Which version could you check by looking, or by asking someone who was there?
  why: A claim that can be checked says what happened, where and when.
O: Put the steps of checking a claim in order.
  items: State the claim exactly | Find where it first came from | Check the date and the place | Look for independent evidence | Say whether it is supported, contradicted or unsettled
  ends: First | Last
  hint: You need to know what the claim is before you can trace it.
  why: Pin it down, trace it, check its context, look for confirmation, and then report honestly what you found.
G: Independent evidence, or a copy of the same source?
  bin Independent: a reporter who visited the pond today | the park authority's own measurement | a dated photograph taken by a resident
  bin A copy: a post quoting the article | a second post quoting the first post | a website that reprints the article
  hint: Did they find it out for themselves, or pass on what someone else said?
  why: Independent sources each have their own access to the facts. Copies add nothing to the original.
K: Three posts say that your town's pond has dried up. Turn over as few facts as you need to decide whether the posts show that it has.
  clue Number: There are three posts, from three different accounts.
  clue Shares: Together they have been shared four hundred times.
  clue Origin: All three link to one article, which is two years old and about a pond in another town.
  clue Neighbour: A neighbour says that ponds do sometimes dry up.
  need: 3
  + The posts do not show it :: Their only source is about another place and another time.
  - The posts show it: three accounts agree :: [repetition-as-confirmation] The three are repeating one source.
  hint: Where did the three posts get their information?
  why: Tracing a claim to its origin often shows that many voices are one voice, and sometimes that the origin is about something else.
>P: The statement "70% of teenagers do this" appears on fifty websites. How should it be checked?
  + Trace it to the original study: who measured it, when, how, and among whom :: Fifty repetitions are not fifty confirmations.
  - It needs no checking: fifty sites say it :: [repetition-as-confirmation] They may all be copying one another.
  - It cannot be checked :: [gave-up] A number has an origin that can be looked for.
  hint: Where did the number first come from?
  why: A statistic is only as good as the study behind it. Find the study, and read how the number was produced.
done: Pin it down, trace it back, and count the sources, not the copies. Trace one claim that you see today to its origin.

@ media.2.visual-story
goal: Explain how framing, cropping, captions and sequence change what an image seems to say.
intro: A photograph records one view at one moment. The frame decides what is in the picture and what is left out. A crop, a caption or the order of the pictures can change what viewers understand, without changing a single thing in the image.
  show: table Choice | What it changes ;; framing | what is inside the picture, and what is outside ;; cropping | how much of the scene remains ;; caption | what viewers are told that they are seeing ;; sequence | what seems to have led to what
demo: A close crop shows three people standing in a crowded-looking corner. The wider frame shows the same three people in an almost empty hall. A caption on the crop saying "the hall was packed" claims far more than the picture can support.
  show: pair text The close crop: three people, shoulder to shoulder || text The wide frame: three people in an empty hall
C: The same moment, framed in two ways. What does the comparison show?
  left: text A close crop: three people in a corner.
  right: text The wide frame: the same three people in an almost empty hall.
  + Cropping removed the context, and a caption about a "packed hall" would not be supported :: The frame chooses what we see.
  - The crop is a fake :: [crop-as-fabrication] Nothing in it was altered. It was selected.
  - Both pictures say the same thing :: [framing-ignored] One suggests a crowd, and the other shows an empty hall.
  hint: What does the wide frame show that the crop conceals?
  why: A true image can still mislead by what it leaves outside the frame.
E: Rewrite the caption for the cropped photograph so that it claims only what the picture supports.
  tokens: Caption: | The whole hall was packed.{Three people talk in a corner of the hall.,A huge crowd filled the hall.}
  limit: 1
  aim: Change the caption so that it describes what the picture actually shows.
  accept: 2=Three people talk in a corner of the hall.
  x 2=A huge crowd filled the hall. :: [overstated-caption] The picture shows three people. It does not show a crowd.
  hint: Describe only what is visible.
  why: A caption should keep the context, and claim no more than the image shows.
G: A record, a reconstruction or an imagined scene?
  bin Record: a photograph of a flood taken on the day | a still from a security camera
  bin Reconstruction: an artist's drawing of a dinosaur, based on its fossils | a diagram of how an accident may have happened
  bin Imagined: a cartoon of a talking river | a drawing for a fairy tale
  hint: Was it captured as it happened, built up from evidence, or made up?
  why: Readers need to know which kind of image they are looking at. Each supports different claims.
P: A photograph of a boy holding a ball is followed by a photograph of a broken window. What do viewers assume, and are they right to?
  + They assume that the boy broke the window, though neither picture shows it :: The sequence suggests a cause.
  - They assume nothing :: [sequence-ignored] People read pictures in order as a story.
  - The pictures prove that he broke it :: [sequence-as-proof] The order implies it. Nothing in either image shows it.
  hint: What story does the order tell?
  why: Placing images in sequence creates a story of cause and effect that no single image contains.
>P: A news site illustrates today's local rain with a dramatic photograph of a storm in another country, without saying so. What should it do?
  + Label the picture as an illustration from elsewhere, or use a photograph of the actual event :: Readers should know whether an image is a record.
  - Nothing: it is only a picture :: [image-as-decoration] Readers will take it for a record of today's weather.
  - Never use pictures at all :: [overcorrection] Pictures are valuable when they are honestly labelled.
  hint: What will readers believe that the picture shows?
  why: An image presented without a label is read as a record of the event. An honest caption says what it really is.
done: Ask what is outside the frame. Take two photographs of one scene that tell different stories.

@ media.3.journalism
goal: Match each claim in a report to a source able to support it, tell attribution from verification, and weigh evidence in editorial decisions.
intro: Reporting turns a question into a documented account. Sources differ in what they know at first hand and in what interests they have. Attribution tells readers who said something. Verification checks whether it is true. A good report keeps observation, attribution and interpretation apart.
  show: table Kind of statement | Example ;; observation | "At 8:35, twelve people were waiting at the stop." ;; attribution | "The operator said that roadworks had caused delays." ;; interpretation | "The service appears to be getting worse."
demo: A report on a delayed bus route uses passengers' accounts, the timetable and the operator's records. A passenger can describe her own morning. A claim that the route is late on most days needs records covering many days. The report keeps what one passenger experienced apart from the measured pattern.
  show: table Claim | A source that can support it ;; this morning's bus was late | a passenger who was on it ;; the route is late on most days | records over several weeks
M: Match each claim to a source that could support it.
  pairs: The 8:15 bus was twenty minutes late this morning => A passenger who was on it | The route is late on most days => The operator's records over several weeks | A bus is promised every ten minutes => The published timetable | The council plans to change the route => The council's minutes, or a named official
  hint: Who is in a position to know each of these?
  why: A source supports a claim only if it has access to that kind of fact.
G: Observation, attribution or interpretation?
  bin Observation: At 8:35, twelve people were waiting at the stop. | The timetable at the stop listed a bus at 8:15.
  bin Attribution: The operator said that roadworks had caused delays. | A passenger said that she had been late for work three times.
  bin Interpretation: The service appears to be getting worse. | The delays suggest that the route is short of buses.
  hint: Did the reporter see it, did someone say it, or is it a conclusion?
  why: Readers should be able to tell what was witnessed, what was claimed and what was concluded.
P: A report says, "The operator said that 95% of its buses run on time." Has the reporter verified the figure?
  + No: it has been attributed, not verified; the records would have to be checked :: Quoting a claim is not confirming it.
  - Yes: the operator said so :: [attribution-as-verification] An interested party's statement is a claim.
  - Yes: 95% is a precise figure :: [precision-as-accuracy] A precise number can be wrong.
  hint: Who is the source of the figure, and does it have an interest?
  why: Attribution reports who said something. Verification is a separate step that tests it.
C: Two ways of handling a well-documented finding and an unsupported assertion that contradicts it.
  left: text Give each the same space, and let readers decide.
  right: text Report both, and make plain which of them has evidence behind it.
  + The right: fairness means examining claims, not treating strong and weak evidence as equal :: False balance misleads.
  - The left: equal space is always fair :: [false-balance] It tells readers that the evidence is evenly divided when it is not.
  - Report only the finding, and conceal the assertion :: [suppression] Readers may meet the assertion elsewhere. It is better examined than hidden.
  hint: What would readers conclude from equal space?
  why: Editorial judgement weighs evidence. Presenting unequal evidence as equal is a distortion.
>P: A reporter has one anonymous source who says that a bus company falsified its punctuality records. There are no documents. What should the editor decide?
  + Not to publish it as fact: look for documents or a second independent source, and put the claim to the company :: A serious claim needs strong evidence.
  - Publish at once: it is a big story :: [scoop-over-evidence] A single unverified source is too weak for an allegation of wrongdoing.
  - Drop the story for good :: [gave-up] It is a lead worth investigating, and is not yet a story.
  hint: What would turn the claim from an allegation into something established?
  why: The more serious the claim, the stronger the evidence required. One anonymous account is a starting point.
done: Who saw it, who said it, and who checked it? Read one news report and mark each sentence as observation, attribution or interpretation.

@ media.3.platforms
goal: Explain how a platform's ranking decides what you see, how your behaviour feeds back into it, and what measures of attention do and do not show.
intro: A platform shows you a tiny selection of what is available. A ranking system chooses it by predicting something, often how long you will watch or whether you will click. The top of your feed is whatever best fits that objective. It is not what is most accurate or most important.
  show: flow signals: your clicks and watching > a prediction > the ranking > what you see > your clicks again
demo: An invented feed ranks posts by predicted viewing time. A sensational clip holds people's attention, though it tells them almost nothing. It rises to the top. Its position reflects the objective the system was given, and is no evidence that the clip is accurate or worth anyone's while.
  show: table Post | Predicted viewing time | Informative? ;; a sensational clip | long | hardly ;; a careful explanation | short | very
M: Match each term to its meaning.
  pairs: Ranking => Ordering items by a predicted score | Signal => The data used to make the prediction | Objective => What the system is built to maximise | Feedback loop => What you click shapes what you are shown, which shapes what you click | Incentive => What creators gain by matching the objective
  hint: The objective is the goal that the system was given.
  why: These terms describe how a feed is assembled, and why it drifts.
X: An invented feed starts with half its posts on one topic. Each round, you click a little more on that topic, and the feed shows you more of it.
  param n: 0..10 /1 @0 | Rounds of clicking and re-ranking
  out share: 100/(1+exp(-0.5*n)) | Share of the feed on that topic | %
  aim: Find how many rounds it takes for the topic to fill at least 90% of the feed.
  target: share >= 90 and share < 93
  view: bars share over n
  hint: Each round builds on the one before.
  why: After five rounds the topic fills 92% of the feed. A slight preference, fed back again and again, becomes a feed that shows little else.
G: What do measures of attention capture?
  bin Measured: the number of views | the minutes watched | the number of shares
  bin Not measured: whether the content is accurate | whether viewers learned anything | whether viewers were glad afterwards to have watched
  hint: Can it be counted automatically from clicks and time?
  why: Measures of attention count behaviour. They say nothing about truth or value, or about what people would choose on reflection.
P: The sensational clip is ranked first in the feed. What does its position tell you?
  + That it scored highest on the system's objective, predicted viewing time :: Nothing about accuracy.
  - That it is the most accurate post :: [ranking-as-quality] Accuracy was not what the system was asked to maximise.
  - That it is the most important post :: [ranking-as-importance] Importance was not measured.
  hint: What was the feed told to maximise?
  why: A ranking reflects its objective and its signals. To know what a position means, ask what the system is optimising.
>P: Creators on a platform notice that angry headlines are ranked higher. What is likely to follow, and what could change it?
  + More angry headlines will be produced, because the measure rewards them; a different objective, or signals of quality, could shift that :: Measures shape what is supplied.
  - Nothing: creators do not respond to rankings :: [ignored-incentives] Creators compete for position, and adapt to what earns it.
  - Angry headlines will disappear by themselves :: [self-correction-assumed] As long as they are rewarded, they will multiply.
  hint: What does the ranking reward?
  why: A metric is an incentive. What a platform measures, it tends to get more of.
done: Ask what the feed is optimising. Notice which kinds of post you are shown most, and why that might be.

@ media.4.theory
goal: Distinguish the questions asked by different theories of communication, and turn a theoretical concept into something that can be measured.
intro: Theories of communication direct attention to different parts of one process. A transmission model asks whether a message got through. Agenda-setting asks whether the prominence of an issue in the media affects what the public thinks important. Framing asks how the way an issue is presented shapes how it is understood.
  show: table Theory | Its question ;; transmission | did the message arrive intact? ;; agenda-setting | does prominence in the media shape what the public thinks important? ;; framing | how does the presentation organise the way an issue is understood?
demo: Two outlets both report on housing very often. One stresses whether people can afford a home. The other stresses housing as an investment. That both give it prominence is a matter for agenda-setting. That they organise it around different ideas is a matter of framing.
  show: table Outlet | Amount of coverage | Emphasis ;; first | high | affordability ;; second | high | investment
G: Which theory asks each question?
  bin Transmission: Did the warning reach the audience without distortion? | Which channel delivers the message fastest?
  bin Agenda-setting: Do the issues given most coverage become those that the public names as most important? | Did a rise in coverage come before a rise in public concern?
  bin Framing: Does describing housing as "an investment" and not as "shelter" change what readers think should be done? | Does calling a policy a "war" change support for it?
  hint: Is it about delivery, about prominence or about presentation?
  why: Each theory generates a different kind of question, and needs a different kind of evidence.
P: A researcher wants to measure how "prominent" an issue is in the news. Which is a workable measure?
  + The number of front-page stories on it in each week :: Countable, and defined in advance.
  - How important the researcher feels it to be :: [subjective-measure] A feeling cannot be checked or repeated by anyone else.
  - Whether the issue really is important :: [concept-not-measure] That is another concept altogether, and not a measurement of prominence.
  hint: Which could two researchers count, and agree on?
  why: To operationalise a concept is to state an observable measure that others can apply in the same way.
E: Turn this vague research question into one that can be tested.
  tokens: When coverage of housing doubles, does it | affect people{raise the share of readers who name housing as the top issue,influence society}
  limit: 1
  aim: Change the question so that it names a measurable outcome.
  accept: 2=raise the share of readers who name housing as the top issue
  x 2=influence society :: [still-vague] "Influence society" names nothing that could be measured.
  hint: What would you count in order to find out?
  why: A testable question states the outcome precisely enough for it to be measured.
P: An outlet earns most of its income from advertisements aimed at property investors. How might that bear on its coverage of housing?
  + Its funding may shape which stories and which frames are chosen; that is a hypothesis to test by comparing outlets with different funding :: Institutions shape production.
  - Not at all: journalists simply report the facts :: [institutions-ignored] What is covered, and how, is produced within organisations that have interests.
  - It proves that the outlet is dishonest :: [overclaimed] It suggests a hypothesis. It does not establish wrongdoing.
  hint: Who pays for the coverage, and what do they want readers to see?
  why: Media content is produced under institutional conditions: ownership, funding and routine. Their influence is a question for evidence.
>P: Two outlets give climate change equal space. One presents it as an economic cost, and the other as a matter of public health. Which concepts describe the likeness and the difference?
  + The same prominence, a matter of agenda-setting; a different presentation, a matter of framing :: Two separate dimensions.
  - Both are differences of framing :: [prominence-as-framing] The amount of coverage is the same. Only the presentation differs.
  - Both are differences of agenda-setting :: [framing-as-prominence] They give the issue equal weight, and frame it differently.
  hint: What is the same about the two, and what is different?
  why: How much an issue is covered, and how it is presented, are distinct. Theories of each ask different questions.
done: Delivery, prominence, presentation: which question are you asking? Compare how two outlets frame one story.

@ media.4.methods
goal: Design a study of media content or of audiences: define the sample and the unit, separate reliability from validity, and match the method to the claim.
intro: Content analysis studies messages systematically, using explicit categories. Before coding anything, the researcher defines the population of interest, the list from which items are drawn, the unit to be coded and the rules. What audiences make of the content is a different question, which needs a different method.
  show: table Decision | Meaning ;; population | all the items that the study is about ;; sampling frame | the list from which items are actually drawn ;; unit of analysis | the thing that is coded: a story, a paragraph, a post ;; codebook | the written rules for each category
demo: A study codes 100 invented news stories for whether they mention the cost of housing. The rules distinguish a passing mention from a central focus. A second coder independently codes 50 of the stories, and agrees with the first on 45. A separate survey asks readers what they noticed.
  show: table Check | Result ;; stories coded by both coders | 50 ;; stories on which they agreed | 45 ;; agreement | 90%
M: Match each term to its meaning.
  pairs: Population => All the items that the study is about | Sampling frame => The list from which items are actually drawn | Unit of analysis => The thing that is coded | Codebook => The written rules for each category | Intercoder agreement => How consistently two coders apply the rules
  hint: The frame is what you can actually draw from, which may be less than the whole population.
  why: Every conclusion from a content analysis depends on these definitions.
F: Calculate the agreement between coders.
  frame: Two coders agree on 45 of 50 stories. Their agreement is __ %
  answer: 90
  x 45 :: [count-as-percentage] Forty-five is the count. Divide it by 50.
  x 10 :: [counted-disagreements] Ten per cent is the share on which they disagreed.
  hint: 45 ÷ 50.
  why: 45 ÷ 50 = 90%. This shows that the rules can be applied consistently.
P: The coders agree 90% of the time on the category "mentions the cost of housing". Does that show that the category captures concern about affordability?
  + No: agreement shows that the rule is applied consistently, not that it measures the intended idea :: Reliability is not validity.
  - Yes: high agreement means a good category :: [reliability-as-validity] Coders can agree perfectly on a category that misses the point.
  - Agreement between coders is meaningless :: [overcorrection] It is necessary. It is simply not sufficient.
  hint: A passing mention of prices and a story about affordability are both "mentions".
  why: Reliability concerns consistency. Validity concerns whether the category means what the researcher intends.
G: Which claims can content analysis support, and which need research on audiences?
  bin Content analysis: Stories about housing doubled between two years. | Most stories quoted officials, and few quoted tenants.
  bin Research on audiences: Readers became more worried about housing. | Readers misunderstood the headline.
  hint: Is the claim about the messages, or about the people who received them?
  why: Analysing content tells you what was said. What people made of it has to be studied in the people.
K: A study describes itself as an analysis of "national news coverage of housing". Turn over as few facts as you need to decide whether its sample supports that description.
  clue Sample: The sample is 100 stories from one newspaper's website, in one month.
  clue Agreement: The coders agreed on 95% of the stories.
  clue Codebook: The codebook runs to twelve pages.
  clue Software: The coding was recorded in a spreadsheet.
  need: 1
  + No: one outlet in one month cannot stand for national coverage :: The frame is far narrower than the claim.
  - Yes: the coding was careful and reliable :: [reliability-as-coverage] Careful coding of a narrow sample is still a narrow sample.
  hint: From what was the sample drawn?
  why: A sample can support claims only about the frame from which it was drawn. The quality of the coding cannot widen it.
>P: A content analysis finds that 80% of stories about a group are negative. The report concludes, "This coverage causes prejudice." What is the criticism?
  + The study describes the coverage; a claim about its effect on audiences needs evidence about audiences :: Content is not effect.
  - None: negative coverage plainly causes prejudice :: [content-as-effect] That is plausible and untested. It is a claim about readers, who were not studied.
  - Content analysis is useless :: [dismissed-the-method] It answers the descriptive question well.
  hint: Who was studied: the stories, or the readers?
  why: Each method supports its own kind of claim. A claim about effects on people requires data on people.
done: Define the frame, the unit and the rules, and claim only what they support. Find the sampling frame of one study of media content.

@ media.5.causal-research
goal: Design and criticise a causal study of media effects: separate exposure from outcome, the effect of assignment from the effect of treatment, and what was shown from what is claimed.
intro: Research on media effects has to keep four things apart: whether something was shown to a person, whether they attended to it, how they understood it, and what it changed. Random assignment of exposure can identify an effect. But it identifies the effect of what was assigned, in the setting studied.
  show: flow exposure > attention > interpretation > outcome
demo: Volunteers are assigned at random to one of two invented styles of headline. Of 500 assigned to style A, 150 go on to share the story. Of 500 assigned to style B, 100 do. Some in each group never read their headline at all. Comparing the groups as they were assigned gives 30% against 20%: the effect of being assigned a style.
  show: table Assigned style | People | Shared | Share ;; A | 500 | 150 | 30% ;; B | 500 | 100 | 20%
F: Calculate the effect of assignment from the table.
  frame: The difference in sharing between the two assigned groups is __ percentage points
  answer: 10 | ten
  x 50 :: [difference-of-counts] Fifty is the difference between the counts. Compare the shares: 30% and 20%.
  x 1.5 :: [ratio-for-difference] That is the ratio. The question asks for the difference.
  hint: 30% less 20%.
  why: 30 − 20 = 10 percentage points. This is the effect of being assigned to style A and not to style B.
P: Some of the volunteers never read their assigned headline. Why not compare only those who did read it?
  + Those who read were not chosen at random, so comparing them breaks the randomisation that made the groups alike :: Readers select themselves.
  - There is no reason not to: it would give a cleaner answer :: [per-protocol-as-causal] It would compare two self-selected groups.
  - Because those who did not read should be punished :: [irrelevant-reason] The reason is statistical: to preserve comparability.
  hint: What made the two groups comparable in the first place?
  why: Analysing people as they were assigned preserves the comparison. It estimates the effect of assignment, which is smaller than the effect of actually reading.
G: Which stage of the process is each?
  bin Exposure: The headline appeared in the person's feed.
  bin Attention: The person looked at it for three seconds.
  bin Interpretation: The person took it to mean that prices will rise.
  bin Outcome: The person's stated trust in the outlet a week later.
  hint: Was it shown, was it noticed, how was it understood, and what changed?
  why: "Media exposure" is not one variable. A study should say which stage it measures.
P: Platform data show that people who see more political content hold stronger views. Why can those data alone not show that the content caused the views?
  + People choose what to view, and the system then shows them more of what they choose :: Selection, and feedback.
  - They can: the two rise together :: [association-as-causation] Strong views may be what led to the viewing.
  - Because platform data are always false :: [dismissed-the-data] They are accurate as records of behaviour. The difficulty lies in the inference.
  hint: Which came first: the views, or the viewing?
  why: In observational platform data, what people see reflects what they already prefer. Without assignment, cause and selection are entangled.
S: Select every practice that belongs in ethical research on media effects.
  + Informed consent, or an approved waiver :: Participants' agreement, or an independent judgement that it may be waived.
  + Materials that pose little risk :: Limiting possible harm.
  + Debriefing when anything was concealed :: Participants are told afterwards what was done.
  + Collecting only the data that are needed :: Limiting exposure of private information.
  - Secretly altering people's feeds in order to upset them :: [covert-harm] That inflicts harm without consent.
  - Publishing participants' individual browsing histories :: [exposed-individuals] That exposes private behaviour.
  hint: What protects the people being studied?
  why: The ethics of research turn on consent, on keeping harm small, on honesty afterwards and on protecting private data.
>P: In a laboratory, students are shown one invented headline, and their attitude is measured immediately afterwards. The paper concludes that "online news polarises society". What is the criticism?
  + The claim goes far beyond the design: one artificial exposure, an immediate measure and student volunteers say little about society over time :: State the scope.
  - None: the experiment was randomised :: [internal-validity-as-generality] Randomisation supports the effect in that setting, and no further.
  - Experiments on media are pointless :: [dismissed-the-method] They answer narrow questions well.
  hint: Who was studied, with what material, and for how long?
  why: An experiment identifies an effect under its own conditions. Generalising to society needs evidence from other people, other settings and longer periods.
done: Shown, noticed, understood, changed, and for whom? Read the limitations section of one study of media effects.

@ media.5.governance-capstone
goal: Design and evaluate an intervention in the governance of a platform: define the goal, weigh unequal errors and build in accountability.
intro: Platform governance decides how rules, ranking, moderation and appeals distribute opportunities and risks. Any intervention must first say what it is for. Reducing the reach of harmful material, improving what people understand and preserving participation are three different goals, and they can conflict.
  show: table Goal | A measure ;; reducing harmful reach | views of flagged posts ;; improving understanding | the share of readers who interpret a label correctly ;; preserving participation | the number of people still posting, and appeals upheld
demo: An invented platform tests context labels on uncertain claims. Clicks on the labelled posts fall. But some readers now take every unlabelled post to be verified. Reach has been reduced, and understanding may have been made worse. The evaluation needs measures of comprehension, an analysis by community and a way to correct mistaken labels.
  show: flow labels are added > fewer clicks on labelled posts > unlabelled posts are taken to be true
G: Which goal does each measure serve?
  bin Reducing harmful reach: the number of views of flagged posts
  bin Improving understanding: the share of readers who can say what a label means | accuracy on a follow-up question
  bin Preserving participation: the number of people who keep posting | the share of appeals that succeed
  hint: Is it about how far something spreads, what people understand, or who can still take part?
  why: An intervention can succeed on one goal and fail on another. Each needs its own measure.
F: A moderation tool reviews 1,000 posts, of which 100 really break the rules. It flags 120 posts, and 80 of those really do break them.
  frame: The number of posts wrongly flagged is __
  answer: 40 | forty
  x 20 :: [counted-the-misses] Twenty is the number of rule-breaking posts that were missed. The wrongly flagged are 120 − 80.
  x 80 :: [counted-the-hits] Eighty were correctly flagged.
  hint: Of the 120 flagged, how many did not break the rules?
  why: 120 − 80 = 40 legitimate posts flagged in error, while 100 − 80 = 20 rule-breaking posts were missed.
P: The tool wrongly flags 40 legitimate posts, and misses 20 that break the rules. Why can these two kinds of error not simply be added together?
  + They harm different people in different ways: one silences legitimate speech, and the other leaves harm in view :: The errors are asymmetric.
  - They can be: an error is an error :: [symmetric-errors] Their costs differ, and they fall on different people.
  - Only the missed posts matter :: [one-error-only] Wrongful removal also does harm, and it often falls on particular communities.
  hint: Who is affected by each kind of mistake?
  why: A wrongful flag and a missed violation have different victims. Governance has to decide the balance between them openly.
O: Put the stages of an accountable evaluation in order.
  items: Define the goal, and the harm, precisely | Try the intervention on a sample | Measure comprehension as well as clicks | Analyse the effects by language and community | Provide appeal and correction | Publish the results and revise
  ends: First | Last
  hint: The goal comes first, and publication and revision come last.
  why: A defined goal, a contained trial, measures matched to the goal, attention to who is affected, a route of correction and openness to scrutiny.
C: Two ways of judging the context labels. Which is defensible?
  left: text Judge them by the fall in clicks alone.
  right: text Judge them by clicks, by comprehension and by their effects on different communities.
  + The right: clicks measure one goal, and the labels may be damaging another :: Several goals need several measures.
  - The left: clicks are objective :: [single-metric] Objective, and blind to what readers now believe.
  - Neither: labels cannot be evaluated :: [gave-up] They can be, against stated goals.
  hint: What did the demonstration show that clicks would miss?
  why: A single measure invites optimising that measure. The side-effects appear only in the others.
>P: Labels cut clicks on labelled posts by 20%. Readers, however, now trust false posts that carry no label more than they did before. What should the platform conclude?
  + The net effect is unclear: the labels have moved the problem, and should be redesigned and tested again, with comprehension measured :: An unintended effect has to be weighed.
  - The labels are a success: clicks fell :: [single-metric] The gain on labelled posts may be cancelled by the loss on unlabelled ones.
  - Labels should be abandoned for good :: [overcorrection] The finding points to a flaw in the design, which can be tested and corrected.
  hint: What happened to the posts that did not get a label?
  why: An intervention changes how people read everything else on the page. Evaluation must look for effects beyond the items treated, and must plan for revision.
done: Name the goal, count both kinds of error, and plan to be corrected. Ask of one platform rule: what is it for, and who bears its mistakes?
